{"id": "2025-01-01-wmo-70pct-5yr-above-1-5c", "result": "unverifiable", "evidence": [{"doc": 1, "quote": "One or more individual years exceeding 1.5°C does not mean that we have missed the goal of “holding the increase in the global average temperature to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels and pursuing efforts to limit the temperature increase to 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels, recognizing that this would significantly reduce the risks and impacts of climate change” as stated in the Paris Agreement. This refers to an extended period, typically decades or longer, although the Agreement itself does not provide a specific definition."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/wmo-confirms-2024-warmest-year-record-about-155degc-above-pre-industrial-level", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11041, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:38:20+00:00", "sha256": "18754b9c8da2e8d659dc35e827fe3a536c48a619cc2f38871f33d18a2661e3bf"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260104210303id_/https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/wmo-confirms-2024-warmest-year-record-about-155degc-above-pre-industrial-level", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11295, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:39:05+00:00", "sha256": "fb45f0ccc73ef7ddd7027b55f76391ce570b6408184434a688bcb0e0bdd3a3a4"}]}
{"id": "2025-04-01-carbon-brief-china-co2-first-clean-energy-decline", "result": "corrected", "evidence": [{"doc": 3, "quote": "15 May 2025 0:01"}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "For the first time, the growth in China’s clean power generation has caused the nation’s carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions to fall despite rapid power demand growth."}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "The new analysis for Carbon Brief shows that China’s emissions were down 1.6% year-on-year in the first quarter of 2025 and by 1% in the latest 12 months."}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "The reduction in China’s first-quarter CO2 emissions in 2025 was due to a 5.8% drop in the power sector. While power demand grew by 2.5% overall, there was a 4.7% drop in thermal power generation – mainly coal and gas."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://www.carbonbrief.org/analysis-clean-energy-just-put-chinas-co2-emissions-into-reverse-for-first-time/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 30205, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:39:05+00:00", "sha256": "28167f8b8bae9fa00403c326e6ebe55c1db762a67bec980793479d95e933057f"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260107230627id_/https://www.carbonbrief.org/analysis-clean-energy-just-put-chinas-co2-emissions-into-reverse-for-first-time/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 36432, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:41:18+00:00", "sha256": "11a34e17a7cb308c291f3600dbe46ab5a3847de703e8e1bd8dd7ae714af07030"}, {"doc": 3, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250514234347id_/https://www.carbonbrief.org/analysis-clean-energy-just-put-chinas-co2-emissions-into-reverse-for-first-time/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 36093, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:41:26+00:00", "sha256": "a6706b5f2f6f6b912c5eda1183b9fb98d97aad9846bda7ff13c4166094e1b18e"}]}
{"id": "2025-06-25-noaa-mcb-ozone", "result": "verified", "evidence": [{"doc": 1, "quote": "June 25, 2025\nDownload Cover Image\nA potential strategy to cool the planet—known as Marine Cloud Brightening (MCB)—could have a significant impact on the stratosphere and the ozone layer, according to a new study led by researchers at NOAA’s Chemical Sciences Laboratory (CSL)."}, {"doc": 1, "quote": "The findings were surprising: MCB deployment at this scale changed atmospheric composition and circulation throughout the troposphere and stratosphere, which increased ozone in some regions of the atmosphere while decreasing it in others."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://research.noaa.gov/marine-cloud-brightening-may-cool-the-earth-but-could-impact-the-ozone-layer/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 9518, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:41:29+00:00", "sha256": "0260d7609f20a341ddf927a56fb71946420e566ebe6f660afd3ace88a3ed06d4"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250919214536id_/https://research.noaa.gov/marine-cloud-brightening-may-cool-the-earth-but-could-impact-the-ozone-layer/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 9500, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:42:50+00:00", "sha256": "00f6a9d49616c89597de0d06a3fd96868e7992693ec3e39f07aa62a32ee5fc49"}, {"doc": 3, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250719022833id_/https://research.noaa.gov/marine-cloud-brightening-may-cool-the-earth-but-could-impact-the-ozone-layer/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 9532, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:42:58+00:00", "sha256": "10643e0c8540f459c6590f48ddd18489cc98ea975a23b98735307e6a0dee5222"}]}
{"id": "2025-07-01-florida-sb56-geoengineering-ban", "result": "corrected", "evidence": [{"doc": 1, "quote": "CS/CS/SB 56: Geoengineering and Weather Modification Activities"}, {"doc": 1, "quote": "Geoengineering and Weather Modification Activities; Repealing provisions relating to the definitions, purpose, licensing requirements, applications, proof of financial responsibility requirements, license issuance and discipline provisions, publication of notice of intention to operate requirements, required contents of the notice of intention, publication of the notice of intention requirements, proof of publication requirements, record and reports of operations requirements, provision of emergency licenses, and suspension or revocation of licenses, respectively, of the weather modification law; prohibiting certain acts intended to affect the temperature, the weather, or the intensity of sunlight within the atmosphere of this state; requiring an operator of public infrastructure to report certain information monthly to the Department of Transportation, etc."}, {"doc": 1, "quote": "Effective Date: 7/1/2025"}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://www.flsenate.gov/Session/Bill/2025/56", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11491, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:45:32+00:00", "sha256": "6b1022ef9a7c09d8e481d200f5cfef697359eec9d2b29d6116423473d68165cd"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260116020647id_/http://flsenate.gov/Session/Bill/2025/56", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11480, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:46:06+00:00", "sha256": "811ba4772d9a5ca0a3628e57532ac56dc6cf4345d4ff259968d41e8e0a86214d"}]}
{"id": "2025-10-24-stardust-raises-60m", "result": "verified", "evidence": [{"doc": 2, "quote": "A once-outlandish idea for reversing global warming took a major step toward reality Friday when Israeli-U.S. startup Stardust Solutions announced the largest-ever fundraising round for any company that aims to cool the Earth by spraying particles into the atmosphere."}, {"doc": 2, "quote": "Its plan to limit the sun’s heat raised $60 million from a broad coalition of investors that included Silicon Valley luminaries and the Agnelli family, an Italian industrial dynasty."}, {"doc": 2, "quote": "Stardust Solutions, a geoengineering company, plans to test its particles inside a plane while flying in the stratosphere in April 2026."}, {"doc": 2, "quote": "By Corbin Hiar and Karl Mathiesen10/24/2025 09:00 AM EDTUpdated: [2025-10-31T13:02:12.801-04:00]"}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/10/24/global-cooling-startup-raises-60-million-dollars-to-test-sun-reflecting-technology-00620340", "status": 403, "ok": false, "chars": null, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:46:06+00:00"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20251228221222id_/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/10/24/global-cooling-startup-raises-60-million-dollars-to-test-sun-reflecting-technology-00620340", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 9525, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T16:47:33+00:00", "sha256": "0904547a5c10accacff9f1e719d2e610240876548d093404dcddc85b11a0ae9a"}]}
